    "body": "> This is just for `querySelectorAll()`? Why not use a normal class? I don't understand the need for prefixing it.\r\n> \r\n> If you want more custom attributes, that's #2271.\r\n\r\nWell, then you clearly didn't face that problem :-)\r\nIn a nutshell, the problem is:\r\nFor example, you need to create, say, a button. When you click it something happens, say, text become green for 1s and then reverts back\r\n```html\r\n<button class=\"button\"></button>\r\n<span class=\"text\"></span>\r\n```\r\nThen you adding some JS (I'll use some jQuery though) like:\r\n```js\r\n$('.button').click(function () { ... });\r\n```\r\nWell, now you simultaneously:\r\n1. Cannot add any more buttons since they will implement not only visual, but logical behavior. When you adding button you expect that it will look like `.button`, but behave like just `<button>`, with no overriding events\r\n2. Cannot remove class `.button` from that element without JS stops working\r\n\r\nTherefore you're obligated to invent some class like `.js-button-make-text-green-on-click` or smth like that. And that doesn't cancel the fact that CSS-classes weren't presented for that. CSS-classes should \"paint\" your element, not help JS to find it\r\n\r\np.s. IDK if I made my point clear, actually, I've found [an article describing that case](https://www.freecodecamp.org/news/css-naming-conventions-that-will-save-you-hours-of-debugging-35cea737d849/#1-use-js-class-names) and also heard about that on an offline-lecture, but can't formulate it by myself :-(",
    "author_association": "NONE",
