    "created_at": "2023-07-27T08:34:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-07-27T08:34:23Z",
    "body": "> You can do this today without issue. HTML specs do not make use of `js-` prefixed attributes, and are highly unlikely to ever do so. So write your `<div js-selector=\"foo\">` and call `document.querySelector('[js-selector=\"foo\"]')`.\r\n\r\n`Highly unlikely` is not good enough. If there is even slightest chances of smth to happen - it will happen, and **when** it will - your web might became broken because of your 20-years old layuots have some attributes that updated modern specification handles in a way you didn't assume",
